              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>18</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_CTRL</name>
          <description>LSC: lens shading correction control.
Multiplies each Bayer sample by a gain interpolated from a mesh, correcting the lens's corner falloff. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x400</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Lens Shading Correction Enable
1 = apply the mesh gains, 0 = pass through unchanged.
Has no effect unless a profile has been loaded and validated -- see LSC_PROFILE_STATUS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_MESH_NODES</name>
          <description>LSC: mesh geometry, shared by all profiles.
The reset value of zero is deliberately invalid: a mesh must be described before it can be loaded, and zero nodes cannot be. Global mesh shape, outside TUNE_BANK. Change only after full drain, leaving profile mode and releasing all sealed resident pins; invalidates mesh confirmations. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x404</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MESH_NX</name>
              <description>Mesh Horizontal Node Count
Legal range 2..32, not exceeding active image width. Cells partition width-1 across nx-1 intervals; integer spacing may differ by one sample. Geometry changes invalidate resident profiles. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MESH_NY</name>
              <description>Mesh Vertical Node Count
Legal range 2..32, not exceeding active image height. Cells partition height-1 across ny-1 intervals; integer spacing may differ by one line. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_STRENGTH</name>
          <description>LSC: how much of the mesh correction to apply.
Blends between no correction and the full mesh, so one calibration can be backed off without reloading it. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x408</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STRENGTH</name>
              <description>Correction Strength
UQ1.16. Legal range 0..0x10000: zero gives unity and 0x10000 applies the full mesh. Larger representable codes extrapolate correction; negative effective gain clamps the pixel to zero. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>17</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_PROFILE_SEL</name>
          <description>LSC: which resident profile the pipeline uses.
Three profiles are held so a different illuminant can be selected without a reload. Legacy mode: selecting an unloaded profile is refused and sets LSC_ERROR.profile_sel_reject. Profile mode: a bank scalar stored without check; SEAL rejects code 3 (INVALID) or an enabled selection of an invalid resident (NOT_READY). [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x40c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PROFILE_SEL</name>
              <description>Active Profile Select
0..2. Code 3 is invalid: rejected at the write in legacy mode, rejected by SEAL in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_LOAD_CTRL</name>
          <description>LSC: sequential mesh loading into an inactive profile. Issue LOAD_BEGIN, write exactly 4*nx*ny coefficients, then LOAD_VALIDATE. Command priority is ABORT, VALIDATE, BEGIN. Begin while busy or validate while idle sets LOAD_PROTOCOL. Profile mode additionally sets TUNE_ERROR.LOCKED for every rejected destination (sealed, pending or in-flight); an in-flight destination sets both LOCKED and ACTIVE_LOAD_REJECT. [access=mixed]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x410</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD_PROFILE</name>
              <description>Load Destination Profile
Destination profile for the load, 0..2.
Loading the active profile is refused with ACTIVE_LOAD_REJECT. Active means selected by an admitted, unretired frame with LSC enabled, or in legacy mode the LSC_PROFILE_SEL profile while LSC_CTRL.EN=1 and ACTIVE_VALID=1. After reset no profile is active. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD_BEGIN</name>
              <description>Begin Coefficient Load
Write 1 to start a load; self-clearing. [access=w1s]</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD_VALIDATE</name>
              <description>Validate Coefficient Load
Write 1 to finish a load; self-clearing.
Checks the coefficient count and marks the profile valid only if it matches. [access=w1s]</description>
              <bitOffset>9</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD_ABORT</name>
              <description>Abort Coefficient Load
Write 1 to discard the partial load and return to idle; self-clearing. The destination remains invalid until reloaded and validated. The active profile is unchanged. [access=w1s]</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_COEF_DATA</name>
          <description>Node gain UQ4.18 [21:0]; unity 0x40000, maximum 8x (0x200000), ISP-UPD-001. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x414</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEF_DATA</name>
              <description>Node gain UQ4.18 [21:0]; unity 0x40000, maximum 8x (0x200000), ISP-UPD-001. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>22</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_LOAD_STATUS</name>
          <description>LSC: progress of a load in flight, read-only. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x418</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD_ADDR</name>
              <description>Coefficients Accepted
Number of coefficients accepted so far in this load. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD_FULL</name>
              <description>Load Complete
1 = the expected 4*nx*ny coefficients have been written. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD_BUSY</name>
              <description>Load In Progress
1 = a load is in progress. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD_PROFILE</name>
              <description>Profile Being Loaded
Profile the load in progress is targeting. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>18</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_PROFILE_STATUS</name>
          <description>LSC: which profiles hold valid data, read-only. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x41c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PROFILE_VALID</name>
              <description>Per-Profile Valid Flags
One bit per resident profile; 1 = loaded and validated.
bit 0 = PROFILE_0
bit 1 = PROFILE_1
bit 2 = PROFILE_2 [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACTIVE_PROFILE</name>
              <description>Profile In Use
Profile the pipeline is currently using. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACTIVE_VALID</name>
              <description>Active Profile Valid
1 = the active profile holds valid data.
If this is 0 the correction is not applied however LSC_CTRL.en is set. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>6</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GEOMETRY_VALID</name>
              <description>Mesh Geometry Valid
1 = LSC_MESH_NODES describes a mesh the block can use. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>7</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_ERROR</name>
          <description>LSC: sticky error flags.  Write 1 to clear.
Sticky because a load is a long sequence: an error part-way through must survive until software finishes and checks. [access=w1c]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x420</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ACTIVE_LOAD_REJECT</name>
              <description>Load Targeted Active Profile
A load targeted the profile currently in use. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROFILE_SEL_REJECT</name>
              <description>Invalid Profile Selected
LSC_PROFILE_SEL selected an invalid or unloaded profile. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>COEF_RANGE</name>
              <description>Coefficient Out Of Range
A written coefficient exceeded the legal gain range 0..4 inclusive in UQ3.18, or LOAD_VALIDATE found such a coefficient in the load. The load stays invalid until the next LOAD_BEGIN or LOAD_ABORT; clearing this bit does not make it valid. Sticky W1C; a simultaneous new error wins over clear. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>COEF_COUNT</name>
              <description>Coefficient Count Mismatch
At validate, the number of coefficients written did not equal 4*nx*ny, or a write after LOAD_FULL was discarded. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GEOMETRY_INVALID</name>
              <description>Invalid Mesh Geometry
The mesh geometry is outside the supported node counts. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LOAD_PROTOCOL</name>
              <description>Load Protocol Violation
The load sequence was violated -- data written with no load open or after LOAD_FULL, or validate without begin. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_STAT_OVF_CNT_R</name>
          <description>LSC: red samples driven to saturation by the correction.
Measured over the completed frame.  A large count means the mesh gain is lifting highlights past full scale. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x424</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OVF_CNT_R</name>
              <description>Red Saturated Sample Count
Saturated red sample count for the last completed frame. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>21</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_STAT_OVF_CNT_GR</name>
          <description>LSC: green-on-red-row samples driven to saturation by the correction. Measured over the completed frame.  A large count means the mesh gain is lifting highlights past full scale. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x428</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OVF_CNT_GR</name>
              <description>Green-Red Saturated Sample Count
Saturated green-on-red-row sample count for the last completed frame. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>21</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_STAT_OVF_CNT_GB</name>
          <description>LSC: green-on-blue-row samples driven to saturation by the correction. Measured over the completed frame.  A large count means the mesh gain is lifting highlights past full scale. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x42c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OVF_CNT_GB</name>
              <description>Green-Blue Saturated Sample Count
Saturated green-on-blue-row sample count for the last completed frame. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>21</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>LSC_STAT_OVF_CNT_B</name>
          <description>LSC: blue samples driven to saturation by the correction.
Measured over the completed frame.  A large count means the mesh gain is lifting highlights past full scale. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x430</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OVF_CNT_B</name>
              <description>Blue Saturated Sample Count
Saturated blue sample count for the last completed frame. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>21</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>BPC_CTRL</name>
          <description>BPC: bad pixel correction control.
Replaces pixels that disagree with their same-colour neighbours. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x600</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Bad Pixel Correction Enable
1 = detect and correct, 0 = pass through unchanged. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>BPC_MODE</name>
          <description>BPC_MODE: reserved compatibility control. The MAS specifies spatial BPC with immediate correction and no cross-frame defect history. Writes retain the field value but do not enable temporal detection. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x604</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DYNAMIC_DET_EN</name>
              <description>Dynamic Detection Enable (reserved)
No effect on the spatial BPC behavior specified by this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>BPC_TEMPORAL_VAR</name>
          <description>BPC_TEMPORAL_VAR: reserved compatibility control. The MAS specifies spatial BPC with immediate correction and no cross-frame defect history. Writes retain the field value but do not enable temporal detection. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x608</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x100</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TEMPORAL_VAR</name>
              <description>Inter-Frame Value Tolerance (reserved)
No effect on the spatial BPC behavior specified by this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>BPC_THRESH</name>
          <description>BPC: detection threshold.
A pixel is a candidate when it differs from its neighbourhood average by more than max(THRESH_FLOOR, THRESH_K * activity), where activity is the spread of the eight same-colour neighbours -- a relative term so textured areas are not over-corrected, and an absolute floor that always applies. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x60c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x4d0192</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>THRESH_K</name>
              <description>Relative Detection Slope
Relative slope, UQ8.8; 256 = 1.0.  Reset 77 = 0.30.  0 disables the relative term and leaves the floor. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>THRESH_FLOOR</name>
              <description>Absolute Detection Floor
Absolute threshold in pipeline (12-bit) code units, always applied. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>BPC_PIXEL_AGE</name>
          <description>BPC_PIXEL_AGE: reserved compatibility control. The MAS specifies spatial BPC with immediate correction and no cross-frame defect history. Writes retain the field value but do not enable temporal detection. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x610</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x4b0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PIXEL_AGE</name>
              <description>Confirmation Frame Count (reserved)
No effect on the spatial BPC behavior specified by this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>BPC_NUM_CANDIDATES</name>
          <description>BPC: per-frame spatial candidate count, read-only; saturates at 16383. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x614</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CANDIDATES</name>
              <description>Spatial Candidate Count
Candidate count of the most recently completed frame: pixels whose absolute difference from the truncated eight-neighbour average strictly exceeds the floor (THRESH_FLOOR, or the colour FLOOR when TUNE_BPC_CTRL.COLOR_THRESH_EN=1). Published at frame end and stable until the next frame completes; saturates at 16383. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>14</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>BPC_NUM_DEFECTIVE</name>
          <description>BPC: per-frame corrected-pixel count, read-only; saturates at 16383. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x618</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DEFECTIVE</name>
              <description>Corrected Pixel Count
Corrected-pixel count of the most recently completed frame, including factory-listed sites. Published at frame end with BPC_NUM_CANDIDATES and stable until the next frame completes; saturates at 16383. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>14</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>BPC_STATUS</name>
          <description>BPC: sticky status.  Write 1 to clear. [access=w1c]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x61c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CAND_REJECT_OVF</name>
              <description>Candidate Table Overflow (reserved for temporal tracking)
Not asserted by the spatial BPC contract, which carries no defect table between frames. Spatial candidate and corrected-pixel counters saturate silently at 16383; saturation does not set this bit. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>WB_CTRL</name>
          <description>WB: white balance control.
Scales each Bayer channel so a neutral subject reads equal in R, G and B. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x800</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>White Balance Enable
1 = apply the channel gains, 0 = pass through unchanged. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>WB_GAIN_R</name>
          <description>WB: red channel gain. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x804</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x100</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GAIN_R</name>
              <description>Red Channel Gain
UQ4.8 for R only; 0x100 = 1.0. Legacy mode uses WB_GAIN_G for both greens; profile mode uses WB_GAIN_G for Gr and TUNE_WB_GB for Gb. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>WB_GAIN_G</name>
          <description>WB: green gain; both greens in legacy mode, Gr in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x808</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x100</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GAIN_G</name>
              <description>Green Channel Gain
UQ4.8, 0x100 = 1.0. Legacy mode applies this gain to Gr and Gb. Profile mode applies it to Gr; TUNE_WB_GB independently controls Gb. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>WB_GAIN_B</name>
          <description>WB: blue channel gain. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x80c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x100</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GAIN_B</name>
              <description>Blue Channel Gain
UQ4.8 for B only; 0x100 = 1.0. Legacy mode uses WB_GAIN_G for both greens; profile mode uses WB_GAIN_G for Gr and TUNE_WB_GB for Gb. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>DG_CTRL</name>
          <description>DG: digital gain control.
A single multiplier on every channel, applied after white balance.  Unlike sensor analogue gain this cannot improve signal-to-noise -- it amplifies the noise with the signal -- so prefer raising the sensor's gain first. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0xa00</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Digital Gain Enable
1 = apply the gain, 0 = pass through unchanged. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>DG_GAIN</name>
          <description>DG: global digital gain. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0xa04</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x100</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GAIN</name>
              <description>Global Digital Gain
UQ4.8; 0x100 = 1.0.  Maximum 0xFFF is just under 16x. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>D_WDR_CTRL</name>
          <description>D_WDR: reserved for a future function. Register storage is present; writes have no effect on image processing in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc00</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Digital WDR Enable (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>D_WDR_STRENGTH</name>
          <description>D_WDR: tone compression strength.
NOT IMPLEMENTED -- see D_WDR_CTRL. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc04</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STRENGTH</name>
              <description>Tone Compression Strength (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>DEMOSAIC_STATUS</name>
          <description>DEMOSAIC: block status. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0xe00</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>The Demosaic Block Busy
1 while the block holds in-flight pixels. Follow the block-specific configuration and LUT loading sequence; busy alone does not grant permission to rewrite live tables. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CTRL</name>
          <description>CCM: colour correction matrix control.
Converts the sensor's native colour response to the working colour space by a 3x3 matrix plus per-channel offset.  The matrix is not optional in the datapath -- clearing the enable applies the identity, it does not remove the block's latency. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1000</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Colour Matrix Enable
1 = apply the matrix, 0 = pass through unchanged. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UPDATED</name>
              <description>Reserved Update Control
Readable/writable compatibility field. Configuration is captured at accepted SOF; no explicit update command is required. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CRR</name>
          <description>CCM: matrix coefficient (0,0), red from red. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1004</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x200</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEFRR</name>
              <description>Matrix Coefficient Red-from-Red
Signed two's-complement Q3.9, 12 bits. Unity is 0x200; range -4.0 through 4.0-1/512. Offsets use the same fractional scale as the accumulator and are added before the final shift. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CRG</name>
          <description>CCM: matrix coefficient (0,1), red from green. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1008</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEFRG</name>
              <description>Matrix Coefficient Red-from-Green
Signed two's-complement Q3.9, 12 bits. Unity is 0x200; range -4.0 through 4.0-1/512. Offsets use the same fractional scale as the accumulator and are added before the final shift. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CRB</name>
          <description>CCM: matrix coefficient (0,2), red from blue. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x100c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEFRB</name>
              <description>Matrix Coefficient Red-from-Blue
Signed two's-complement Q3.9, 12 bits. Unity is 0x200; range -4.0 through 4.0-1/512. Offsets use the same fractional scale as the accumulator and are added before the final shift. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CGR</name>
          <description>CCM: matrix coefficient (1,0), green from red. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1010</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEFGR</name>
              <description>Matrix Coefficient Green-from-Red
Signed two's-complement Q3.9, 12 bits. Unity is 0x200; range -4.0 through 4.0-1/512. Offsets use the same fractional scale as the accumulator and are added before the final shift. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CGG</name>
          <description>CCM: matrix coefficient (1,1), green from green. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1014</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x200</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEFGG</name>
              <description>Matrix Coefficient Green-from-Green
Signed two's-complement Q3.9, 12 bits. Unity is 0x200; range -4.0 through 4.0-1/512. Offsets use the same fractional scale as the accumulator and are added before the final shift. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CGB</name>
          <description>CCM: matrix coefficient (1,2), green from blue. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1018</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEFGB</name>
              <description>Matrix Coefficient Green-from-Blue
Signed two's-complement Q3.9, 12 bits. Unity is 0x200; range -4.0 through 4.0-1/512. Offsets use the same fractional scale as the accumulator and are added before the final shift. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CBR</name>
          <description>CCM: matrix coefficient (2,0), blue from red. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x101c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEFBR</name>
              <description>Matrix Coefficient Blue-from-Red
Signed two's-complement Q3.9, 12 bits. Unity is 0x200; range -4.0 through 4.0-1/512. Offsets use the same fractional scale as the accumulator and are added before the final shift. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CBG</name>
          <description>CCM: matrix coefficient (2,1), blue from green. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1020</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEFBG</name>
              <description>Matrix Coefficient Blue-from-Green
Signed two's-complement Q3.9, 12 bits. Unity is 0x200; range -4.0 through 4.0-1/512. Offsets use the same fractional scale as the accumulator and are added before the final shift. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_CBB</name>
          <description>CCM: matrix coefficient (2,2), blue from blue. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1024</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x200</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>COEFBB</name>
              <description>Matrix Coefficient Blue-from-Blue
Signed two's-complement Q3.9, 12 bits. Unity is 0x200; range -4.0 through 4.0-1/512. Offsets use the same fractional scale as the accumulator and are added before the final shift. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_OFS_R</name>
          <description>Signed 12-bit offset in output pixel levels; code 1 adds one level (ISP-UPD-007). [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1028</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OFFSET_R</name>
              <description>Signed 12-bit offset in output pixel levels; code 1 adds one level (ISP-UPD-007). [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_OFS_G</name>
          <description>Signed 12-bit offset in output pixel levels; code 1 adds one level (ISP-UPD-007). [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x102c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OFFSET_G</name>
              <description>Signed 12-bit offset in output pixel levels; code 1 adds one level (ISP-UPD-007). [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_OFS_B</name>
          <description>Signed 12-bit offset in output pixel levels; code 1 adds one level (ISP-UPD-007). [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1030</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OFFSET_B</name>
              <description>Signed 12-bit offset in output pixel levels; code 1 adds one level (ISP-UPD-007). [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CCM_STATUS</name>
          <description>CCM: block status, read-only. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1034</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>Colour Matrix Busy
1 while the block holds in-flight pixels. Follow the block-specific configuration and LUT loading sequence; busy alone does not grant permission to rewrite live tables. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GAMMA_CTRL</name>
          <description>GAMMA: enable and explicit SRAM load confirmation. Hard reset and common soft reset in profile mode clear validity; legacy common soft reset preserves it. Writes invalidate the destination. Load and verify the complete curve before confirmation, following the selected control-mode ownership rules. [access=mixed]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1200</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Gamma enable: apply the frame-selected curve only after LUT_LOADED is set; otherwise pass through unchanged. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GAMMA_VALUE</name>
              <description>Curve Preset Select (reserved)
Readable/writable compatibility field with no processing effect. Software constructs the curve and loads it through the LUT port. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LUT_LOAD_CONFIRM</name>
              <description>Confirm all 256 entries written and verified while Gamma is disabled and idle. Reads zero. Any LUT write invalidates the table and dominates a coincident confirmation. [access=w1p]</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>write-only</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GAMMA_LUT_ADDR</name>
          <description>GAMMA: LUT index.
Auto-increments on every write to GAMMA_LUT_DATA, so a full curve is loaded by writing the address once and then 256 data values. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1204</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ADDR</name>
              <description>Gamma LUT Index
Entry index 0..255.  Entry i is the output for input i/255 of full scale; the 8 index bits are the top 8 of each 12-bit component. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GAMMA_LUT_DATA</name>
          <description>GAMMA: LUT write port.
The same value is written to all three channel tables -- the hardware has no per-channel curve. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1208</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DATA</name>
              <description>Gamma LUT Write Data
12-bit output level, 0..4095.
Reading this register returns the last value written, not the memory contents; use GAMMA_LUT_RDATA to read the curve back. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GAMMA_LUT_RDATA</name>
          <description>GAMMA: table contents at GAMMA_LUT_ADDR, read-only. Address reads do not increment the index. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x120c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDATA</name>
              <description>Gamma LUT Read Data
12-bit contents of the selected entry. Memory contents are undefined before initialization; the reset-value column does not initialize RAM. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GAMMA_STATUS</name>
          <description>Gamma status. LUT_LOADED reports confirmed validity of curve bank 0 in both modes; bank-0 writes, INIT of bank 0, leaving profile mode, hard reset and profile-mode common soft reset clear it. An unloaded table bypasses correction. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1210</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>The Gamma Block Busy
1 while the block holds in-flight pixels. Follow the block-specific configuration and LUT loading sequence; busy alone does not grant permission to rewrite live tables. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LUT_LOADED</name>
              <description>Software-confirmed validity of curve bank 0 in both modes; legacy confirmation and profile-mode GAMMA_CONFIRM of bank 0 set it. INIT of bank 0 and leaving profile mode also clear it. Hard reset and profile-mode common soft reset clear it; legacy common soft reset preserves it. Verify the complete curve before confirmation. An unloaded table bypasses Gamma. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CSC_CTRL</name>
          <description>CSC: RGB to YCbCr conversion, no bypass. STD selects BT.601 or BT.709; TUNE_CSC_CTRL.FULL_RANGE selects range independently, default full range. Optional look controls and wide intermediate arithmetic follow the frame profile. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1400</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STD</name>
              <description>Colour Standard Select
0 BT.601 (reset), 1 BT.709. Held for the frame. Range is independently selected by TUNE_CSC_CTRL.FULL_RANGE, default full range. No bypass. CH2 inverse CSC uses matching frame-held standard and range. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GTM_CTRL</name>
          <description>GTM: automatic tone adaptation from a completed prior frame or a protected manual curve. Automatic updates are at least one frame delayed and may wait for curve completion and ownership; manual mode holds the software-selected curve. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1600</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Tone Mapping Enable
1 = apply the tone curve, 0 = pass through unchanged. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MANUAL</name>
              <description>Manual Curve Hold
1 holds automatic curve construction. Load the software curve with EN=0 and MANUAL=1, verify it, then enable. MANUAL does not permit memory writes while EN=1. Legacy mode applies manual bank 0 only when all 65 points are complete; otherwise samples pass through. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GTM_LUT_ADDR</name>
          <description>GTM: ratio LUT index.
Auto-increments on every write to GTM_LUT_DATA. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1604</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ADDR</name>
              <description>Tone Curve Index
Control point 0..64.  The table is 65 entries, not 64: sixty-four interpolation intervals plus the endpoint, which is why the field is 7 bits. Point i covers luma 4*i. Data writes at 65..127 are discarded and read back zero. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GTM_LUT_DATA</name>
          <description>GTM: ratio LUT write port. Memory updates only while GTM_CTRL.EN=0; enabled writes do not update the table. MANUAL=1 holds automatic construction during loading and use. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1608</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DATA</name>
              <description>Tone Curve Write Data
UQ8.8 ratio applied to that luma level; 256 = unity, below darkens, above brightens.  Reading returns the shadow, not the table -- use GTM_LUT_RDATA. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GTM_KEY</name>
          <description>GTM: photographic key scales scene-referred luminance for automatic tone-curve construction; it does not guarantee the output arithmetic mean. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x160c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x2e</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>KEY</name>
              <description>Photographic Key
UQ0.8 photographic key. Reset 0x2E = 46/256 = 0.1796875, approximately 0.18. This is the automatic curve exposure parameter, not a guaranteed output mean. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GTM_LWHITE</name>
          <description>GTM: the luminance that maps to white.
Everything above this is compressed to full scale.  The hardware forms the reciprocal itself. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1610</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x400</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LWHITE</name>
              <description>White Point Luminance
UQ8.8 relative to the scene average; reset 0x0400 = 4.0. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GTM_ROI_LOG2</name>
          <description>GTM: centered metering window with nominal edge 2^n. Each axis independently clamps its exponent to floor(log2(active extent)), so the effective window may be rectangular. Power-of-two area permits shift normalization. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1614</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0xb</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ROI_LOG2</name>
              <description>Metering Window Size (log2)
Nominal edge 2^n, independently clamped to powers of two fitting the input width and height. Reset 11. Statistics are measured on input luma and the resulting automatic curve is used on a later frame. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>5</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GTM_LUT_RDATA</name>
          <description>GTM: ratio LUT read-back at GTM_LUT_ADDR, read-only. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1618</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDATA</name>
              <description>Tone Curve Read Data
Profile mode: the TUNE_BANK-selected bank manual curve at GTM_LUT_ADDR; entries without both byte lanes written read zero; automatic curves are not readable. Legacy mode: manual bank 0 when MANUAL=1 (incomplete entries read zero), the most recently published automatic curve when MANUAL=0. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>GTM_STATUS</name>
          <description>GTM: block status. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x161c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>The Tone-Mapping Block Busy
1 while the block holds in-flight pixels. Follow the block-specific configuration and LUT loading sequence; busy alone does not grant permission to rewrite live tables. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>NR_2D_CTRL</name>
          <description>NR_2D: spatial noise reduction control.
A Haar-wavelet denoise whose strength follows a noise variance measured on the previous frame. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1800</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>2D Noise Reduction Enable
1 = filter, 0 = pass through unchanged.
This block routes the stream around the engine when disabled, so the change is held until the engine has drained rather than applied mid-frame. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>NR_2D_EST_VAR</name>
          <description>NR_2D: read-only automatic noise-variance estimate from a completed frame. Automatic mode consumes an eligible prior estimate; manual and luma-LUT modes select their configured variance instead. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1804</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EST_VAR</name>
              <description>Measured Noise Variance
NR_EST_VAR: unsigned 16-bit integer variance, saturated to 65535; reset zero. Internal sigma is unsigned UQ9.8 (17 bits) before squaring and shifting. This readback is the automatic estimate, independent of the selected variance mode. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>NR_2D_STATUS</name>
          <description>NR: block status. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1808</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>The 2D Noise-Reduction Block Busy
1 while the block holds in-flight pixels. Follow the block-specific configuration and LUT loading sequence; busy alone does not grant permission to rewrite live tables. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TNR_3D_CTRL</name>
          <description>TNR_3D: reserved for a future temporal noise-reduction function. Register storage is present; writes have no image-processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1a00</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Temporal NR Enable (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TNR_3D_MTF_SEL</name>
          <description>TNR_3D: motion transfer function preset.
NOT IMPLEMENTED -- see TNR_3D_CTRL. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1a04</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MTF</name>
              <description>Motion Transfer Preset (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS.
0 = none, 1 = weak, 2 = medium, 3 = strong, 4 = aggressive. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>TNR_3D_HIST_BASE</name>
          <description>TNR_3D: DDR base address of the history frame.
NOT IMPLEMENTED -- see TNR_3D_CTRL. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1a08</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>HIST_BASE_ADDR</name>
              <description>History Frame Base Address (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_CTRL</name>
          <description>EE: edge enhancement control.
Sharpens by adding back a clamped, gain-shaped high-pass of the luma at two scales. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c00</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Edge Enhancement Enable
1 = sharpen, 0 = delayed pass-through (the latency is unchanged). [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>REG_UPDATE</name>
              <description>Reserved Update Control
Readable/writable compatibility field; no explicit commit command. Enable, selected profile and scalar configuration are captured at the accepted window-centre SOF. In profile mode, the ingress token supplies the bank enable and resident selection; local SOF uses that captured token. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROFILE_SEL</name>
              <description>Active Parameter Profile Select
0..2 select one of three resident tuning profiles. Captured at accepted window-centre SOF. Code 3 selects profile 0. Separate from PROFILE_WR_SEL. In profile mode, the ingress token supplies the bank enable and resident selection; local SOF uses that captured token. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROFILE_WR_SEL</name>
              <description>Live resident programming/readback selector 0..2. Code 3 discards writes and reads profile 0. Not banked by TUNE_BANK. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_ALPHA</name>
          <description>EE: fine-scale (3x3) sharpening strength. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c04</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x4000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ALPHA</name>
              <description>Fine-Scale Sharpening Strength
UQ1.15; 0x8000 = 1.0 and is the maximum.
Larger values are clamped to 1.0 by the hardware rather than wrapping. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_BETA</name>
          <description>EE: coarse-scale (5x5) sharpening strength. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c08</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x2000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BETA</name>
              <description>Coarse-Scale Sharpening Strength
UQ1.15; 0x8000 = 1.0 and is the maximum, clamped as ALPHA is. The coarse term broadens the halo; usually set below ALPHA. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_CLAMP</name>
          <description>EE: limits on how far one pixel may be moved.
The asymmetry is deliberate -- overshoot on the bright side of an edge is more visible than undershoot, so the positive clamp is usually the smaller. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c0c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x1810</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CLAMP_POS</name>
              <description>Positive Correction Limit
Largest positive correction, in 8-bit luma counts. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CLAMP_NEG</name>
              <description>Negative Correction Limit
Largest negative correction, in 8-bit luma counts. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_FEATURE_EN</name>
          <description>EE: optional activity, contrast and radial gain modulation. With all three disabled, correction still uses ALPHA/BETA and the always-applied luma LUT; disabled optional modulations contribute unity. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c10</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ACTIVITY_EN</name>
              <description>Activity Modulation Enable
1 = modulate by local activity, from the ACT LUT.
Suppresses sharpening in flat areas, where it would only amplify noise. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CONTRAST_EN</name>
              <description>Contrast Modulation Enable
1 = modulate by local contrast, from the CPOS and CNEG LUTs. Separate tables for positive and negative excursions. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RADIAL_EN</name>
              <description>Radial Modulation Enable
1 = modulate by distance from the image centre, using the R2 thresholds and the four radial gains.  Lets the corners be sharpened differently from the centre. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_RADIAL_CENTER</name>
          <description>EE: centre of the radial falloff.
Held at twice the coordinate so the centre can sit on a half-pixel, which matters for an even-sized frame whose true centre is between pixels. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c14</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CENTER_X2</name>
              <description>Radial Centre Column (x2)
Twice the centre column.  For a 1920-wide frame the centre is 1919/2, so this is 1919. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>13</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CENTER_Y2</name>
              <description>Radial Centre Row (x2)
Twice the centre row. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>13</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_RADIAL_R2_TH0</name>
          <description>EE: radial zone boundary 0.
Squared distance, so no square root is needed in the datapath. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c18</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R2_TH0</name>
              <description>Radial Zone 0 Boundary
Squared radius at which zone 0 ends, in units of (2*pixel)^2 to match the doubled centre coordinates. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>27</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_RADIAL_R2_TH1</name>
          <description>EE: radial zone boundary 1.
Squared distance, so no square root is needed in the datapath. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c1c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R2_TH1</name>
              <description>Radial Zone 1 Boundary
Squared radius at which zone 1 ends, in units of (2*pixel)^2 to match the doubled centre coordinates. Must not be below R2_TH0. Zones are tested in order and the first match wins; in profile mode SEAL rejects decreasing thresholds with RADIAL_EN=1 (INVALID). [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>27</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_RADIAL_R2_TH2</name>
          <description>EE: radial zone boundary 2.
Squared distance, so no square root is needed in the datapath. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c20</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>R2_TH2</name>
              <description>Radial Zone 2 Boundary
Squared radius at which zone 2 ends, in units of (2*pixel)^2 to match the doubled centre coordinates. Must not be below R2_TH1. Zones are tested in order and the first match wins; in profile mode SEAL rejects decreasing thresholds with RADIAL_EN=1 (INVALID). [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>27</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_RADIAL_GAIN_01</name>
          <description>EE: radial gains for the innermost two zones. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c24</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x80008000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GAIN0</name>
              <description>Radial Gain, Innermost Zone
UQ1.15 gain applied inside R2_TH0; 0x8000 = 1.0 = no change. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GAIN1</name>
              <description>Radial Gain, Zone 1
UQ1.15 gain applied between R2_TH0 and R2_TH1; 0x8000 = 1.0 = no change. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_RADIAL_GAIN_23</name>
          <description>EE: radial gains for the outer two zones. Resident scalar: PROFILE_WR_SEL selects both read and write; outside TUNE_BANK. Writes clear confirmation and are blocked while active, sealed, pending or referenced in profile mode. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c28</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x80008000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GAIN2</name>
              <description>Radial Gain, Zone 2
UQ1.15 gain applied between R2_TH1 and R2_TH2; 0x8000 = 1.0 = no change. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GAIN3</name>
              <description>Radial Gain, Outermost Zone
UQ1.15 gain applied beyond R2_TH2; 0x8000 = 1.0 = no change. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_LUT_CTRL</name>
          <description>Live EE table selector and resident confirmation port. Scalar or LUT writes clear destination validity. Confirmation requires complete scalars/tables and mode-specific ownership: unreferenced in profile mode, disabled/idle in legacy mode. [access=mixed]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c2c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LUT_SEL</name>
              <description>Gain Table Bank Select
Also selects which bank EE_LUT_RDATA reads.
0x0 = LUMA (Luma table, 64 entries)
0x1 = ACTIVITY (Activity table, 64 entries)
0x2 = CONTRAST_POS (Contrast-positive table, 32 entries)
0x3 = CONTRAST_NEG (Contrast-negative table, 32 entries) [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PROFILE_LOAD_CONFIRM</name>
              <description>Confirm complete resident scalars and four LUTs. Profile mode: destination unreferenced by active/sealed/pending/in-flight state. Legacy: EE disabled/idle. Same-edge scalar/LUT write wins and leaves invalid. [access=w1p]</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>write-only</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_LUT_ADDR</name>
          <description>EE: gain table index.
Auto-increments on every write to EE_LUT_WDATA. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c30</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LUT_ADDR</name>
              <description>Gain Table Index
Entry index.  0..63 for the luma and activity tables, 0..31 for the two contrast tables, where the top bit is ignored. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_LUT_WDATA</name>
          <description>EE: gain table write port. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c34</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LUT_WDATA</name>
              <description>Gain Table Write Data
UQ1.15; 0x8000 = 1.0. Each selected profile owns its tables. RAM contents are undefined after reset: load every required entry before enabling EE. Values above 1.0 clamp on use. Readback uses EE_LUT_RDATA. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_STATUS</name>
          <description>EE status. LUT_VALID reports confirmed profiles. An invalid selected profile bypasses correction without reading undefined SRAM contents. [access=mixed]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c38</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>Block holds in-flight pixels. Profile mode protects active/sealed/pending/referenced residents; another unreferenced resident may be loaded. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERROR</name>
              <description>Radial Gain Range Error
Sticky W1C. Sets once per processed frame when EE and radial gain are enabled and an active-profile radial gain exceeds 1.5 (0xC000). The gain clamps to 1.5 on use. A simultaneous new event wins over clear. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LUT_VALID</name>
              <description>Confirmed resident scalars and four LUTs for profiles 0..2. Scalar/LUT writes or input-domain changes clear destination validity; hard reset and profile-mode common reset clear all. Invalid selected profile bypasses enhancement. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACTIVE_PROFILE</name>
              <description>Resident selection captured for the most recent accepted local EE SOF; does not follow PROFILE_WR_SEL. 0..2; zero after hard/common reset. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ACTIVE_VALID</name>
              <description>One after a local SOF selected a confirmed resident; zero until then or after its validity is cleared. Separate from block busy and enable. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>EE_LUT_RDATA</name>
          <description>EE: gain table readback selected by PROFILE_WR_SEL, EE_LUT_CTRL.LUT_SEL and EE_LUT_ADDR. Programming selector 3 reads profile 0. Contents are undefined until loaded. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1c3c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RDATA</name>
              <description>Gain Table Read Data
Contents of the selected table entry. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CNF_CTRL</name>
          <description>CNF: chroma noise filter control.
A 5x5 filter that averages chroma over neighbours whose luma and chroma are both close enough, so it smooths noise without bleeding colour across edges. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1e00</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Chroma Noise Filter Enable
1 = filter chroma, 0 = pass through unchanged. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UPDATED</name>
              <description>Reserved Update Control
Readable/writable compatibility field with no commit effect. Enable and thresholds are sampled at accepted SOF. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CNF_CHROMA_TH</name>
          <description>CNF: how different a neighbour's chroma may be and still be averaged in. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1e04</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CHROMA_TH</name>
              <description>Chroma Similarity Threshold
Legal range 0..510, in chroma counts. Compared against abs(U-Uc)+abs(V-Vc). Code 511 is reserved, accepted by the register, and behaves as 510. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>9</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CNF_LUMA_TH</name>
          <description>CNF: how different a neighbour's luma may be and still be averaged in. This is what keeps the filter from averaging across an edge. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1e08</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>LUMA_TH</name>
              <description>Luma Similarity Threshold
T_Y, 0..255, in luma counts. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CNF_STRENGTH</name>
          <description>CNF: reserved strength control. The specified filter uses enable and the luma/chroma thresholds; this field has no processing effect. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1e0c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>MODE_STRENGTH</name>
              <description>Filter Strength (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CNF_STATUS</name>
          <description>CNF: block status, read-only. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x1e10</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>Chroma Noise Filter Busy
1 while the block holds in-flight pixels. Follow the block-specific configuration and LUT loading sequence; busy alone does not grant permission to rewrite live tables. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RESIZER_CTRL</name>
          <description>CH0 NV12 output resizer control, maximum output 3840x2160. The unprefixed RESIZER_* registers belong to CH0. Input is the post-IQ crop for this channel; the crop may be larger than the output. No upscaling or custom target sizes. Reject invalid targets before DMA arm; no silent FullRes fallback. Wait for channel OUT_W/H to settle. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2000</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>CH0 resizer enable. 1 selects SCALE; 0 bypasses scaling and preserves crop size. Both paths must fit the 3840x2160 output limit. Change geometry only after stop/drain; validate before DMA arm. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>UPDATED</name>
              <description>Legacy software update hint; readable storage with no commit effect. Active size, crop, enable and SCALE changes trigger geometry derivation. Custom-size words are reserved. Wait for OUT_W/H to settle before arming DMA; capture geometry at SOF. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SCALE</name>
              <description>CH0 NV12 preset selector [7:3]. 0=FullRes crop bypass; 1..14 established presets; 16=640x640, 17=640x384, 18=640x352, 19=512x512, 20=416x416, 21=320x320. Codes 15 and 22..31 reserved. Maximum output 3840x2160. Output must not exceed crop dimensions. No upscaling. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>3</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>5</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RESIZER_CUSTOM_W</name>
          <description>Deprecated target-size address. Reserved in ABI 1.07; reads zero and ignores writes. Programmable custom resizing is not supported. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2004</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESERVED</name>
              <description>Reads zero; writes ignored. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RESIZER_CUSTOM_H</name>
          <description>Deprecated target-size address. Reserved in ABI 1.07; reads zero and ignores writes. Programmable custom resizing is not supported. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2008</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RESERVED</name>
              <description>Reads zero; writes ignored. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RESIZER_OUT_W</name>
          <description>Validated derived output dimension. Reset zero; may retain the last completed value while geometry is invalid. Check the channel GEOMETRY_STATUS before arming DMA. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2014</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OUT_WIDTH</name>
              <description>Validated derived output dimension. Reset zero; may retain the last completed value while geometry is invalid. Check the channel GEOMETRY_STATUS before arming DMA. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>13</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RESIZER_OUT_H</name>
          <description>Validated derived output dimension. Reset zero; may retain the last completed value while geometry is invalid. Check the channel GEOMETRY_STATUS before arming DMA. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2018</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>OUT_HEIGHT</name>
              <description>Validated derived output dimension. Reset zero; may retain the last completed value while geometry is invalid. Check the channel GEOMETRY_STATUS before arming DMA. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>13</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>RESIZER_STATUS</name>
          <description>RESIZER: block status. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x201c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>Resizer Busy
1 while the block holds in-flight pixels. Follow the block-specific configuration and LUT loading sequence; busy alone does not grant permission to rewrite live tables. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>OUTPUT_ENABLE</name>
          <description>Enabled output channels. Defaults to CH0. Accepted SOF snapshots mask at distributor. Enabled channels share lossless backpressure. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2020</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CHANNELS</name>
              <description>Bit 0 Preview NV12, bit 1 Video NV12, bit 2 NPU RGB888. Change only with input stopped and all output paths drained. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>OUTPUT_IRQ_EN</name>
          <description>Per-output interrupt enables. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2024</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DONE</name>
              <description>Mask bit for completion events. Field bits 0..2 correspond to CH0..CH2. One enables interrupt contribution; zero masks it. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERROR</name>
              <description>Mask bit for error events. Field bits 0..2 correspond to CH0..CH2. One enables interrupt contribution; zero masks it. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>OUTPUT_IRQ_STATUS</name>
          <description>Per-output completion and error events; hardware set dominates software clear. Completion waits for write responses. [access=w1c]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2028</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DONE</name>
              <description>Sticky W1C completion events; bit c sets when channel c publishes ODMA_BUF_DONE. Field bits 0..2 correspond to CH0..CH2. Hardware set wins over clear. Clearing the event does not release buffer ownership. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERROR</name>
              <description>Sticky W1C error events; bit c sets when a recovery retires an enabled channel-c output without DONE. Field bits 0..2 correspond to CH0..CH2. Hardware set wins over clear. Clearing the event does not release buffer ownership. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>4</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_CTRL</name>
          <description>AEC: auto-exposure statistics control.
The block only measures -- it does not drive exposure.  Software reads the sums, counts and histogram and decides what to ask the sensor for. [access=mixed]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2200</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>AE Statistics Enable
1 = accumulate statistics, 0 = idle. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>COMMIT</name>
              <description>Configuration Commit
Write 1 to transfer the shadowed enable, zone geometry, clip limits and thresholds at the next accepted ingress SOF. Self-clears on transfer. Active image height is captured per frame and is not shadowed by this command. [access=w1s]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToSet</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_CONTEXT_ID</name>
          <description>AEC: software tag carried with the statistics.
Exposure changes take effect some frames after they are requested, so the tag is how software tells which request a result belongs to: write it here, read it back from AEC_RESULT_CONTEXT_ID with the matching frame. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2204</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CONTEXT_ID</name>
              <description>Exposure Context Tag
Software value captured at ingress SOF and associated with that frame. Write before admitting the frame; publication exposes it in AEC_RESULT_CONTEXT_ID. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_ZONE_CFG</name>
          <description>AEC: zone grid.
The frame is divided into a grid and each zone accumulates independently, so software can weight the metering however it likes. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2208</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x1820</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>NUM_ZONE_X</name>
              <description>Horizontal Zone Count
Horizontal zones, 1..32. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NUM_ZONE_Y</name>
              <description>Vertical Zone Count
Vertical zones, 1..24.  With the maximum grid there are 768 zones. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>5</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_ZONE_SIZE</name>
          <description>AEC: nominal nonzero zone dimensions, 1..4095 each. Require (Nx-1)*zone_width &lt; active_width &lt;= Nx*zone_width and similarly in Y. The last zone covers the remainder. A single full-frame zone is legal; use authoritative 64-bit results rather than narrow low-bit projections. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x220c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x5a0078</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ZONE_WIDTH</name>
              <description>Nominal Zone Width
Nominal zone width in pixels. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ZONE_HEIGHT</name>
              <description>Nominal Zone Height
Nominal zone height in lines. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_SAMPLE_CLIP</name>
          <description>Inclusive RAW12 Bayer sum/count qualification bounds, 0..4095. MIN &lt;= MAX required. Extrema and histograms observe all measured samples independently of these clips. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2210</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0xfff0000</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SAMPLE_MIN_CLIP</name>
              <description>Minimum qualifying RAW12 Bayer value, inclusive; valid range 0..4095; invalid values disable the frame measurement. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAMPLE_MAX_CLIP</name>
              <description>Maximum qualifying RAW12 Bayer value, inclusive; valid range 0..4095; invalid values disable the frame measurement. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_THRESH</name>
          <description>AEC: RAW12 Bayer threshold settings for per-zone over/under counts. OE increments for S&gt;TH_OE; UE increments for S&lt;TH_UE, independently of sum clipping. Any value above 4095 disables that frame measurement. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2214</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0xea00110</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TH_UE</name>
              <description>Strict RAW12 underexposure classification: sample &lt; threshold. Startup 272; programmable and sensor-calibrated. Require UE &lt; OE. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TH_OE</name>
              <description>Strict RAW12 overexposure classification: sample &gt; threshold. Startup 3744; programmable and sensor-calibrated. Require UE &lt; OE. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_STATUS</name>
          <description>AEC: block status. [access=mixed]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2218</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>AE Statistics Busy
1 = a frame is being accumulated. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STAT_DONE</name>
              <description>Statistics Ready
Sticky W1C completion event. Results and identity publish atomically into a READY bank. ACQUIRE before reading; RELEASE after copying. Clearing STAT_DONE acknowledges the event and does not release the bank. Hardware completion wins over simultaneous clear. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERROR</name>
              <description>AE Statistics Error
(not implemented) Reads 0; not set by hardware.  Software detects a missed frame from AEC_FRAME_ID. [access=w1c]</description>
              <bitOffset>2</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <modifiedWriteValues>oneToClear</modifiedWriteValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_FRAME_ID</name>
          <description>AEC: ingress frame identifier of the leased result bank, read-only. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x221c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FRAME_ID</name>
              <description>Shared ingress-SOF frame number of the leased statistics. First admitted frame after hard reset is 1; wraps modulo 2^32. Common and DMA soft resets preserve the counter. Metadata remains stable until RELEASE; no lease returns zero and sets the ownership error. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_RESULT_CONTEXT_ID</name>
          <description>AEC: software context tag published with the leased statistics frame; stable until RELEASE. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2220</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CONTEXT_ID</name>
              <description>Captured photometric configuration epoch for the measured frame. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_CHANNEL_SEL</name>
          <description>Legacy readout selector: 0 aggregate Bayer statistics; 1..3 return zero rather than per-CFA results. This is not the AE source tap selector; use TUNE_AEC_SOURCE. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2224</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CHANNEL_SEL</name>
              <description>Legacy readout selector: 0 aggregate Bayer statistics; 1..3 return zero rather than per-CFA results. This is not the AE source tap selector; use TUNE_AEC_SOURCE. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_GLOBAL_SUM_LO</name>
          <description>AEC: whole-frame qualified RAW12 Bayer sum, low word, read-only. Narrow readback is a low-bit modulo projection from the same acquired lease; use the 64-bit indirect readout for authoritative totals. No-lease reads return zero and set NO_LEASE. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2228</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GLOBAL_SUM_LO</name>
              <description>Frame Sum, Low Word
Bits [31:0] of the qualified sum. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_GLOBAL_SUM_HI</name>
          <description>AEC: whole-frame qualified RAW12 Bayer sum, high bits, read-only; retained width for map compatibility. Narrow readback is a low-bit modulo projection from the same acquired lease; use the 64-bit indirect readout for authoritative totals. No-lease reads return zero and set NO_LEASE. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x222c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GLOBAL_SUM_HI</name>
              <description>Frame Sum, High Bit
Bit [32] of the qualified sum. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_GLOBAL_COUNT</name>
          <description>AEC: number of RAW12 Bayer samples contributing to the qualified global sum, read-only. The count excludes samples outside the inclusive clip bounds. Narrow readback is a low-bit modulo projection from the same acquired lease; use the 64-bit indirect readout for authoritative totals. No-lease reads return zero and set NO_LEASE. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2230</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GLOBAL_COUNT</name>
              <description>Frame qualified RAW12 Bayer sample count. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>23</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_ZONE_ADDR</name>
          <description>AEC: zone read selector, 0..767 in raster order. Narrow results project the same acquired bank as the 64-bit port. Two result banks preserve READY/LEASED data while another frame is collected; never reuse results before RELEASE. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2234</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ZONE_ADDR</name>
              <description>Readout Zone Select
Zone index 0..767, in raster order: y * num_zone_x + x. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_ZONE_SUM</name>
          <description>AEC: qualified RAW12 Bayer sum for the selected zone, read-only. Narrow readback is a low-bit modulo projection from the same acquired lease; use the 64-bit indirect readout for authoritative totals. No-lease reads return zero and set NO_LEASE. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2238</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ZONE_SUM</name>
              <description>Zone Sum
Qualified sum for the zone at AEC_ZONE_ADDR. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>28</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_ZONE_COUNT</name>
          <description>AEC: qualified RAW12 Bayer sample count for the selected zone, read-only; wraps modulo 65,536. Narrow readback is a low-bit modulo projection from the same acquired lease; use the 64-bit indirect readout for authoritative totals. No-lease reads return zero and set NO_LEASE. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x223c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ZONE_COUNT</name>
              <description>Qualified RAW12 Bayer count for selected zone; wraps modulo 65,536. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_ZONE_GREEN_OE_UE</name>
          <description>AEC: legacy register name retained. Fields report RAW12 Bayer under/over counts for the selected zone, independent of clip qualification. Narrow readback is a low-bit modulo projection from the same acquired lease; use the 64-bit indirect readout for authoritative totals. No-lease reads return zero and set NO_LEASE. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2240</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GREEN_UE_COUNT</name>
              <description>Zone Under-Exposed Count
Observed RAW12 Bayer values below TH_UE in this zone; wraps modulo 65,536. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GREEN_OE_COUNT</name>
              <description>Zone Over-Exposed Count
Observed RAW12 Bayer values above TH_OE in this zone; wraps modulo 65,536. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>16</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_ZONE_GREEN_MIN_MAX</name>
          <description>AEC: legacy register name retained. Fields report the leased zone minimum/maximum observed RAW12 Bayer; an empty zone reads min=4095,max=0. Without a lease the word reads zero and sets NO_LEASE. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2244</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>GREEN_MIN</name>
              <description>Zone minimum observed RAW12 Bayer. Empty zone reads 4095; zero without a lease. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>GREEN_MAX</name>
              <description>Zone maximum observed RAW12 Bayer. Empty zone reads 0. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_HIST_ADDR</name>
          <description>AEC: select one of 64 RAW12 Bayer histogram bins; bin n represents Bayer sample values 64n through 64n+63. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2248</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>HIST_ADDR</name>
              <description>Bin index 0..63; bin n covers RAW12 Bayer values 64n..64n+63. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AEC_HIST_DATA</name>
          <description>AEC: population of the selected bin in the 64-bin histogram of all observed RAW12 Bayer samples, read-only. Narrow readback is a low-bit modulo projection from the same acquired lease; use the 64-bit indirect readout for authoritative totals. No-lease reads return zero and set NO_LEASE. [access=ro]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x224c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>HIST_COUNT</name>
              <description>Histogram population. Bin index is normalized RAW12 sample[11:6] (sample &gt;&gt; 6); full-frame count supports up to 3840x2160. [access=ro]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>23</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AWB_CTRL</name>
          <description>AWB: non-blocking post-BPC Bayer-cell statistics. Each accepted 2x2 RGGB cell contributes R, rounded-average green and B once on the uniform cell grid. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2400</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EN</name>
              <description>Enable Bayer-cell AWB measurement; invalid enabled geometry is skipped and reported. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NUM_ZONE_X</name>
              <description>Horizontal zone count over active cell columns (active width/2). [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>1</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NUM_ZONE_Y</name>
              <description>Vertical zone count over active cell rows (active height/2). [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AWB_X_BOUND_ADDR</name>
          <description>AWB_X_BOUND_ADDR: reserved boundary-table control. Uniform boundaries are derived from AWB_CTRL zone counts and active geometry; writes have no processing effect. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2404</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>X_BOUND_ADDR</name>
              <description>Horizontal Boundary Index (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AWB_Y_BOUND_ADDR</name>
          <description>AWB_Y_BOUND_ADDR: reserved boundary-table control. Uniform boundaries are derived from AWB_CTRL zone counts and active geometry; writes have no processing effect. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2408</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>Y_BOUND_ADDR</name>
              <description>Vertical Boundary Index (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>5</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AWB_X_BOUND_DATA</name>
          <description>AWB_X_BOUND_DATA: reserved boundary-table control. Uniform boundaries are derived from AWB_CTRL zone counts and active geometry; writes have no processing effect. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x240c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>X_BOUND_DATA</name>
              <description>Horizontal Boundary Value (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>X_BOUND_WE</name>
              <description>Horizontal Boundary Write Enable (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>AWB_Y_BOUND_DATA</name>
          <description>AWB_Y_BOUND_DATA: reserved boundary-table control. Uniform boundaries are derived from AWB_CTRL zone counts and active geometry; writes have no processing effect. [access=rw]</description>
          <addressOffset>0x2410</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>Y_BOUND_DATA</name>
              <description>Vertical Boundary Value (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>12</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>Y_BOUND_WE</name>
              <description>Vertical Boundary Write Enable (not implemented)
Reserved; no processing effect in this MAS. [access=rw]</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
